1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:03,380
Dating the past and reconstructing environments.

2
00:00:03,842 --> 00:00:09,066
This video presents the explanation accompanying a ninety-minute classroom lesson.

3
00:00:09,526 --> 00:00:14,592
Pause for the exercises and revisit the relevant sections as needed.

4
00:00:15,395 --> 00:00:16,883
Today’s lesson.

5
00:00:17,345 --> 00:00:22,345
Focus on choosing an appropriate method and interpreting what its date means, rather than

6
00:00:22,358 --> 00:00:25,204
memorizing every calculation.

7
00:00:26,007 --> 00:00:27,732
When did it happen?.

8
00:00:28,036 --> 00:00:33,036
Place building repairs, forest changes and climate variation in time.

9
00:00:33,496 --> 00:00:35,299
Which came first?.

10
00:00:35,601 --> 00:00:39,476
Order and time lag affect the explanations we can test.

11
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:42,044
What can the evidence show?.

12
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:46,391
Distinguish simultaneous events from a causal explanation.

13
00:00:46,851 --> 00:00:52,141
To relate environmental change to human activity, we need to know not just what changed but

14
00:00:52,154 --> 00:00:54,848
also which event came first.

15
00:00:55,651 --> 00:00:59,389
Overlapping date ranges can leave the order unresolved.

16
00:00:59,851 --> 00:01:04,640
When date ranges overlap, the order of events may remain unresolved.

17
00:01:05,101 --> 00:01:09,708
Inspect the full ranges rather than only their central values.

18
00:01:10,511 --> 00:01:14,841
Tighter dates help us examine order, time lag and duration.

19
00:01:15,303 --> 00:01:18,388
Tighter dates reduce the possible sequences.

20
00:01:18,842 --> 00:01:25,000
Temporal proximity is useful evidence, but it does not establish a cause by itself.

21
00:01:25,803 --> 00:01:27,639
Understanding history.

22
00:01:28,095 --> 00:01:32,029
Compare documents, objects and structural timbers.

23
00:01:32,489 --> 00:01:34,799
Putting the present in context.

24
00:01:35,253 --> 00:01:40,036
Compare current conditions with variability beyond instrumental records.

25
00:01:40,489 --> 00:01:42,430
Informing decisions.

26
00:01:42,884 --> 00:01:47,693
Use histories of change to inform forest management and preparedness.

27
00:01:48,147 --> 00:01:53,318
Past records extend the perspective beyond short instrumental observations and allow

28
00:01:53,332 --> 00:01:57,380
comparisons with conditions different from those of today.

29
00:01:58,183 --> 00:01:59,769
Relative age.

30
00:02:00,225 --> 00:02:02,619
An older-or-younger relationship.

31
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:05,758
Example: lower and upper strata.

32
00:02:06,212 --> 00:02:09,027
Check for overturning and redeposition.

33
00:02:09,488 --> 00:02:11,179
Numerical age.

34
00:02:11,633 --> 00:02:13,916
An age expressed in years.

35
00:02:14,369 --> 00:02:18,172
Example: a ring formed in 1300 CE.

36
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:22,198
Numerical dates retain assumptions and uncertainty.

37
00:02:22,659 --> 00:02:27,297
Knowing an order differs from assigning a numerical age or calendar date.

38
00:02:27,751 --> 00:02:32,376
Numerical results still have uncertainty that must be reported.

39
00:02:33,179 --> 00:02:37,489
The year a ring formed can differ from the year a building was constructed.

40
00:02:37,944 --> 00:02:42,438
Trace the process connecting the dated material to the event of interest.

41
00:02:42,892 --> 00:02:46,732
The sample and the event need not have the same age.
